# IEx Session Cheatsheet

Use this page to submit, inspect, and debug runs from an interactive Elixir
session while the Docker-free Favn development stack runs in another terminal.
The IEx session is an operator client: it talks to the Orchestrator HTTP API and
does not own durable state or start a second Favn stack.

## Start the two terminals

Start Favn from the consumer project:

```bash
mix favn.dev
```

From the same project directory, start IEx in another terminal:

```bash
iex -S mix
```

Both terminals need the same environment and project checkout. Local calls read
the Orchestrator URL, service token, and workspace ID from `.favn/local`, which
`mix favn.dev` writes. They return `{:error, :not_running}` when that local state
does not exist.

Do not add `:favn_runner` as a direct runtime dependency just to use IEx.
Consumer applications depend on `:favn` and supported plugins. The isolated
runner is started and verified by `mix favn.dev`.

## Run work

Run a pipeline and wait for its terminal result:

```elixir
{:ok, run} = Favn.run(MyApp.Pipelines.Daily)
```

Submit without waiting:

```elixir
{:ok, accepted} =
  Favn.run(MyApp.Pipelines.Daily,
    window: "day:2026-07-27",
    wait: false
  )
```

Run one asset with its upstream dependencies:

```elixir
Favn.run(MyApp.Mart.Orders,
  dependencies: :all,
  refresh: :missing
)
```

Use `dependencies: :none` only for a targeted repair whose upstream inputs have
already been verified. Asset refresh values are `:auto`, `:missing`,
`:force_selected`, `:force_selected_upstream`, and `:force_all`. Pipeline
refresh values are `:auto`, `:missing`, and `:force_all`; pipelines do not
accept `dependencies`.

`Favn.run/2` waits by default. Useful bounded options are `wait_timeout_ms`,
`run_timeout_ms`, `poll_interval_ms`, `retry_max_attempts`, and
`retry_backoff_ms`.

## Inspect runs

List recent runs:

```elixir
{:ok, runs} = Favn.list_runs(limit: 20)
```

Filter by one status:

```elixir
{:ok, failed_runs} = Favn.list_runs(status: :error, limit: 20)
```

Fetch one run and its events:

```elixir
run_id = accepted["id"]

{:ok, run} = Favn.get_run(run_id)
{:ok, events} = Favn.run_events(run_id, limit: 200)
{:ok, later_events} = Favn.run_events(run_id, after_sequence: 200, limit: 200)
```

Responses cross the JSON API boundary, so response maps have string keys. IEx
helpers such as `Map.take/2`, `Enum.filter/2`, and `Enum.group_by/2` are useful:

```elixir
Enum.map(runs, &Map.take(&1, ["id", "status", "target_label", "started_at", "finished_at"]))

Enum.filter(events, fn event ->
  event["event_type"] in ["node_failed", "run_failed"]
end)
```

## Cancel and diagnose

Request cancellation:

```elixir
{:ok, cancellation} = Favn.cancel_run(run_id)
```

Wait for that run to become terminal:

```elixir
{:ok, cancelled_run} =
  Favn.cancel_run(run_id,
    wait: true,
    wait_timeout_ms: 30_000,
    poll_interval_ms: 500
  )
```

Read the bounded runtime diagnostic report:

```elixir
{:ok, report} = Favn.diagnostics()
```

## Public IEx functions

| Function | Result |
| --- | --- |
| `Favn.run(target, opts)` | Submit an asset or pipeline; wait by default. |
| `Favn.list_runs(opts)` | List persisted run summaries. |
| `Favn.get_run(run_id, opts)` | Fetch one persisted run. |
| `Favn.run_events(run_id, opts)` | List one run's persisted events. |
| `Favn.cancel_run(run_id, opts)` | Request cancellation and optionally wait. |
| `Favn.diagnostics(opts)` | Read allowlisted Orchestrator diagnostics. |

These functions return `{:ok, value}` or `{:error, reason}`. During debugging,
bind the whole result before using an exact match so the original error remains
visible:

```elixir
result = Favn.list_runs(limit: 20)

case result do
  {:ok, items} -> Enum.map(items, &Map.take(&1, ["id", "status"]))
  {:error, reason} -> {:inspect_failed, reason}
end
```

Writing `{:ok, runs} = Favn.list_runs(...)` raises `MatchError` when the
operation returns an error, which hides the result you usually want to inspect.

## Connect to another Orchestrator

For a deployed or separately configured Orchestrator, set all three variables
before starting IEx:

```bash
export FAVN_ORCHESTRATOR_URL='https://orchestrator.example'
export FAVN_ORCHESTRATOR_SERVICE_TOKEN='...'
export FAVN_WORKSPACE_ID='workspace-id'
iex -S mix
```

You can instead pass `orchestrator_url`, `service_token`, and `workspace_id` as
options to each function. All three are required together. Do not paste
service tokens into shared IEx history.

## Common failures

| Result | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `{:error, :not_running}` | No local `.favn/local` state was found; start `mix favn.dev` from this project. |
| `{:error, {:incomplete_cli_environment, names}}` | Only part of the three-variable remote connection was configured. |
| `{:error, %{reason: {:http_error, 401, _}}}` | The service token was rejected. |
| `{:error, %{reason: {:http_error, 403, _}}}` | The identity lacks the required workspace role. |
| `{:error, %{reason: {:http_error, 500, _}}}` | The Orchestrator could not complete the read; inspect its logs and `Favn.diagnostics/1`. |
| `runner release verification failed: :runner_release_id_missing` during `iex -S mix` | The consumer is starting the internal `:favn_runner` application. Remove that direct dependency, or mark a temporary dependency-resolution override with `runtime: false`. |

Use the [Local Development](local-development.html) guide for setup and runtime
lifecycle details. Use `mix favn.*` for shell automation and the functions on
this page for interactive exploration.
